<gpx version="1.1" creator="Good Shit" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1"><trk><name>Gangolfsberg basalt circuit from Oberelsbach — Oberelsbach, Elstal, Gangolfsberg basalt and Dünsberg loop [planning only — not navigation-grade]</name><desc>Start in Oberelsbach and take Rathgeberstraße into the meadow approach, then follow the Elstal walking path. At the gravel-road junction go a few metres left, then right uphill. Make the full short excursion to Schweinfurter Haus and return to the main route; refreshment availability must be checked separately. Continue to the external Teufelskeller interpretation-board and cave-viewing ground, return along its short branch, then follow the basalt-wall, chapel and nature-trail circuit.

After descending the quarry stairs, take the short right-hand excursion at their foot and return to the main path. Continue through the Dünsberg grassland and viewing grounds, then close the loop back in Oberelsbach. The Teufelskeller stop is external walking and viewing ground, with no cave-interior visit required.

Waymarking is partial: the original meadow approach is unmarked, and signing varies between the Elstal path, educational trail and connecting grounds. Use a map and sturdy footwear. Steep gradients, uneven basalt, roots and quarry stairs can become slippery when wet or icy. Plan for daylight in spring, summer or autumn, and turn back if weather, footing, visibility or navigation becomes uncertain. This is not a winter cave-entry itinerary.

Stay on the adopted designated walking grounds. Keep dogs leashed, avoid disturbing nesting or resting wildlife, leave plants and fungi untouched, take litter away, and do not light fires, camp, place caches or create shortcuts. Check current local signs, path conditions, lawful restrictions and closures before departure. If any required meadow, stream, House approach, external cave-viewing, nature-trail, quarry-stair or Dünsberg leg is closed, prohibited or unsafe, the whole itinerary is unavailable.

The complete mapped loop is approximately 14.4 km. The inherited 420 m ascent, 420 m descent and 285 minutes remain approximate historical planning values, not surveyed elevations, a measured height profile or a walking-time guarantee. No elevations are assigned. Released topographic mapping describes the course; it is not a current field survey or a guarantee of safe or open ground.

